-- title:
--   Conorm on Pic⁰ is injective and preserves n-torsion
-- statement:
--   Let $K, F, K', F'$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $F'$ a $K'$-algebra, together with algebra maps $K \to K'$, $F \to F'$ and $K \to F'$ compatible in the two towers $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to F'$, and assume $K$ and $K'$ are algebraically closed and $K$ has characteristic zero. Assume further that $F$ contains an element transcendental over $K$ over whose generated subfield $K(x)$ the field $F$ is finite-dimensional, and likewise for $F'$ over $K'$; that $F/K$ and $F'/K'$ both satisfy `IsCurveOver`, i.e. every nonzero function has a principal divisor of degree zero, every place has residue field finite over the constant field, and the module of Kähler differentials is free of rank one; and that the $K'$-subfield of $F'$ generated by the image of $F$ is all of $F'$. Here a place is a valuation subring of the function field, different from the whole field, containing the image of the constant field and a principal ideal ring; divisors are finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued functions on places, degree-zero divisors form the kernel of the degree homomorphism (weighting a place by the dimension of its residue field), and $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is the quotient of the degree-zero divisors by the principal ones. The conclusion asserts the existence of an additive homomorphism $\iota : \mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K) \to \mathrm{Pic}^0(F'/K')$ that is injective; that is pinned down on conorms, in the sense that whenever a degree-zero divisor $D$ on $F/K$ and a degree-zero divisor $D'$ on $F'/K'$ satisfy $D'(v') = D(v)$ for every place $v'$ of $F'$ whose valuation subring contracts along $F \to F'$ to the valuation subring of a place $v$ of $F$, and $D'(v') = 0$ at every $v'$ contracting to no place of $F$, then $\iota$ sends the class of $D$ to the class of $D'$; and that for every $n \neq 0$ the image under $\iota$ of the $n$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)$ (the $\mathbb{Z}$-torsion by $n$) equals the full $n$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F'/K')$.
--
--   This is the rigidity of the torsion of the Jacobian under a constant field extension of algebraically closed fields in characteristic zero: the conorm map identifies $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)[n]$ with $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F'/K')[n]$, the extension $K'/K$ not being required to be algebraic (the intended case being $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ inside a large complete field). It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld treatment of Shimura curve models, where an equivariant conorm map on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ along such an extension is needed.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_exists_injective_conorm_map_torsion_eq_of_isAlgClosed.lean

